# Saroja, Six Annas! / சரோஜா ஆறணா!
Tamil title: சரோஜா ஆறணா!

Those words gave me a violent shock.

It was no hallucination. I heard them distinctly. My very breath faltered. The man who uttered the words that unsettled me wore sacred ash upon his forehead and clean white clothes. He was neither a scoundrel, a destitute wanderer nor a man of confused mind. Yet it was he who said, “Saroja, six annas!”

My tongue would not rise to ask, “What? What?” Nor would my mind permit me to say it was none of my concern. I stood in the middle of the street, greatly troubling the cart drivers. Did that man have no eyes to see my condition? He gave a faint smile.

I went closer. “Saroja, six annas!” What injustice, what outrage! Had the country decayed and civilization perished to such an extent that Saroja could be auctioned in broad daylight on a crowded market street?

Selling a woman in the street! And at that price! That Saroja for six annas?

Unable to restrain my fury, I glared at the man wearing sacred ash.

“What injustice is this, sir? Without the slightest shame you shout, ‘Saroja, six annas!’”

The man grew angry.

“Injustice? Do you know Pankaja’s price today?” he asked. Paying no attention to my trembling agitation, he added, “Pankaja costs ten annas.”

I could bear no more. The following heated exchange took place between us.

“What injustice! You shout ‘Saroja, six annas; Pankaja, ten annas.’ Has the age fallen so low?”

“What am I to do if the age has fallen? Am I responsible for these things? Can I stop the course Saroja and Pankaja take?”

“Even if you cannot stop it, can you not at least refuse to take part in the injustice? To look at you, one would think you a decent man.”

“What is this? Early in the morning I have acquired a madman! Does my taking a share alone offend your eyes? Other men earn thousands and tens of thousands a month in this very trade. I do not even receive a proper quarter-anna commission on each rupee. Think how much I must struggle, shout and walk for it. And you have come to blame me!”

“Why engage in such a shameless livelihood?”

The man raised his hand and struck me.

“Wretch! Did you strike me?” I shouted, and slapped him in return.

A crowd gathered. I gained courage, certain that anyone with pride and a sense of justice would support me. Holding the broker’s arm, I appealed to them:

“Listen to this injustice! Saroja is six annas, he says. He shouts it boldly in the middle of the street.”

“If you do not need it, go away. Why strike him?”

“If you dislike the price, do not buy. Leave. Can you beat him?”

“If it is unjust, go question the government. What gives you the courage to strike this man?”

Such arrows of speech flew from every direction and struck me. My astonishment had no limit. What injustice! Did a man who made his living pricing women in the street enjoy so much support? What manner of city was this? I staggered in bewilderment.

The beaten man spoke plaintively:

“I have moved through this market for ten years, and no one has ever even called me a dog. My unlucky hour brought me a beating from this rogue today. I thought he had come to buy goods, so I quoted the current price. He picked a needless quarrel, dragged me into a fight and struck me.”

He truly sounded pitiable. But was it wrong to strike a monster who sold a woman?

“Was it wrong to hit you? Was your deed not degrading? Selling a woman for six annas in broad daylight, in the middle of the street!” I demanded.

“What? What? Did he sell a woman?” several people cried in alarm.

“Yes. He said, ‘Saroja, six annas.’”

At once many laughed and called me a lunatic.

“You great fool! Did you imagine that when I said ‘Saroja, six annas’ I was quoting a woman’s price? Idiot! I was quoting the price of yarn from Saroja Mill,” said the beaten man.

Mocking laughter erupted. People began blessing me with titles such as “village simpleton” and “king of fools.” Some pulled my upper garment, some tripped my feet and others threw pebbles. Embarrassment dripped from my face. Still unable to grasp the full truth, I began speaking meekly.

“Sir, I understand nothing. I grew angry and struck this man only because I thought he was quoting a woman’s price. Please tell me the truth.”

The beaten man himself explained.

“This is the yarn market, younger brother. It is clear you have never noticed it before. Bales of yarn from many mills are traded here. We are brokers: we quote prices and arrange sales.

“Just when I saw you, a telegram arrived stating that a bundle of Coimbatore Saroja Mill yarn was priced at Rs. 44-6-0. Yesterday it sold for forty-four rupees. ‘Six annas’ meant the price had risen by six annas. I also quoted Pankaja Mill goods at Rs. 44-10-0.

“Thousands earn large sums in this brokerage business. I told you I receive only a quarter-anna commission per rupee. You imagined all of it concerned a woman. My fate! I received a beating from you as well.”

“Sir, forgive me. I thought yarn sales occurred inside shops, not in the middle of the street. Had you shown the bales while conducting business, I would have understood. You held nothing in your hands. When you said ‘Saroja, six annas,’ I was shocked. I thought you were selling some woman and recruiting a buyer. That is why I committed this improper act. Please forgive me.”

The crowd dispersed. I reached the railway station in distress.

Only that day did I learn that Saroja Mill yarn sold at Rs. 44-6-0; that the price caused such excitement; and that brokers roamed the streets on its account. I had imagined yarn bales went directly to yarn shops and weavers bought them there. I did not know men spoke of “Saroja, six annas” and “Pankaja, ten annas.” That day I questioned several more people about the matter.

“Why do you ask? Prices rise outrageously. They have risen nine, ten and twenty-fold. Today’s price will not exist tomorrow; telegrams arrive hour by hour. Many men in the middle gather profits.

“A man orders two hundred bales today at thirty rupees. He is not a weaver and does not need a bale of yarn. But he has money and knowledge of trade. He buys two hundred bales. Within a week the price rises to forty, and his profit account rises with it.

“Prices sometimes fall and some men suffer, but now they keep rising. A man buys boldly. Even if the price appears to fall a little, he holds the goods without fear of loss. Later brokers shout through the streets, ‘Saroja, 44-6-0; Pankaja, 44-10-0.’ The buyer rejoices, puts down his figures and calculates: ‘Five thousand on this item!’”

The following conversation occurs in cloth shops:

“You sold this same piece for Rs. 3-12-0 yesterday.”

“Yes. What is the yarn price today? It has risen four rupees. Come tomorrow and this piece will cost six.”

People buying cloth begin to wonder why nudist societies have not arisen in the country.

All these hardships fall upon people who use the goods, while a handful of men gain enormous profits from the brokerage that lies across the path and obstructs manufactured goods before they reach consumers. Money and profit multiply. But who suffers because sorrow twists the poor person’s face? Where would men sporting with Saroja and Pankaja find time even to look at that face?

I returned to my village enriched by all this experience and knowledge. Even the sight of clothes began to anger me.

“People wander and suffer so much for you! You grant enormous profit to a few and give everyone else only hardship!” I felt like cursing them.

“Did you not see a cinema in the city? Why does your face look so withered?” my wife asked. I did not answer.

“Is that a new sari? What did it cost?” I demanded with a glare.

“An outrageous price. The same sari cost six rupees last month. Now they would not reduce even a coin from eight and a quarter,” my wife replied.

“Because people like you buy whatever price they name, every man keeps raising it as he pleases,” I said angrily.

My wife laughed. “What can we do without buying? What can we women do if those sinners raise the price?” she soothed.

True. Poor Dhanam, my wife, what could she do?

That night she cooled my anger with gentle words.

“Do you know of an injustice? You know Kuppusami in the last house, the elder brother who weaves at the loom. A bright red baby girl was born to him, his sixth child already. What could the poor man do? They sold her for six annas to Akilandam in the next street. Akilandam has no children. She keeps the baby lovingly, fondling her and calling her ‘my eye, my gold.’”

“They sell the child they bore? What misery!”

“It is a sin, but the mother was not heartless. She looked and looked at the child and wept. What could she do? Poverty tears them apart and devours them. Her husband earns ten annas for a full day’s work, they say. At today’s prices that does not provide even gruel. If the baby suffers stomach trouble or fever, there is no money for medicine.

“Earlier, whenever he wove cloth he could sell it immediately at the shop. Now the shopkeeper refuses it. Yarn prices have risen. He cannot buy yarn at that price and weave cloth. When it was affordable, he lacked money to buy a larger stock. The poor weaver suffers.”

Misery for the man who weaves clothing; hardship for the person who buys it. Men with no need for yarn buy it, lock it away and gather profit. What cruelty was this? Speculators drove the yarn price up and down, trapping and crushing the weaver while his family cried. How and when would this hardship end? The thought exhausted me.

At the pot-breaking festival, a pole is coated with clay and oil, and men are made to climb for a little purse tied high above. For every span they ascend, they slip a cubit. Nor is that all. As they climb, people below repeatedly pour water over both pole and climber. It is a game conducted in God’s name.

In just that fashion, the weaver is told to climb the slippery pole called trade and seize the purse called “income” tied at its top. As water is poured upon the festival climber, the broker’s “kindness” rains upon the weaver. The weaver’s life contains more anguish than the struggle of the man climbing that pole.

Seeing me sunk in sorrow as I thought of these things, Dhanam continued:

“It is pitiful. They sold her for six annas. Akilandam has even named the baby Saroja.”

When my wife said this, my body shook without my knowing it.

Saroja, six annas!

There in Madras the words denoted yarn. Here in my village I heard that a weaver’s baby daughter, Saroja, had been sold for six annas because the family could no longer bear its burden.

My heart melted like wax before a flame.

But what use is melting? Does the world notice the tears of the poor?

It does not!

25 April 1943
